#!/usr/bin/env bash
# One tehas cycle for one issue, and its report.
#
# Deterministic, no model. It runs tehas work for the issue and reports the
# outcome on the issue (and the token usage on the pull request). The
# dispatcher starts it as tehas-cycle@<issue>.service, after it has checked who
# labelled the issue and marked it tehas:running. This command checks no
# authorisation, as tehas work checks none: run by hand it runs a cycle for any
# open issue. It does check the number itself: a closed issue and a pull request
# are refused before a label is changed or a container starts.
#
# Usage:
#   tehasbot cycle <issue>
#
# Exit: 0 the cycle ran and was reported, whatever its outcome; 3 a cycle for
# this issue is already running; 64 the argument is not an issue number; 65 the
# number is not work the factory takes: a pull request, a closed issue, or an
# answer GitHub could not give after three tries; 75 the state store was busy
# and did not start the cycle's record after three tries, or timed out on the
# start (a start it took then is ended as refused); 77 the state store
# refused the start: no approval of the dispatcher's is waiting for it, or the
# lock is not held as the store sees it; 78 instance settings are missing or
# malformed, or the state store cannot be used; 70 the cycle's end. 75, 77 and
# 78 come before any label, comment or container.
#
# It holds state/cycles/<issue>.lock for as long as it runs. The dispatcher
# counts the held locks, so a cycle that dies frees its place. Once it holds
# the lock it starts the cycle's record on the state store (lib/store.sh,
# cycle-start): the attempt, when it started, the approval it uses and the
# pi-rukas commit of the sandbox image. The new attempt is what the dispatcher
# waits for. Started by tehas-cycle@.service (TEHAS_CYCLE_FROM=unit, or the
# INVOCATION_ID systemd sets) the start needs the approval the dispatcher
# recorded, and is refused without one. Run by hand it is on record as a run
# by hand and uses no approval. A cycle that then refuses its number ends its
# record as refused: the unit did come up, and the issue keeps tehas:running
# until the next poll clears it.
#
# A TERM, INT or HUP is passed on to tehas work, which stops the container. The
# cycle is then reported as any other that stopped without a pull request, but
# with one bounded try for each call to GitHub and no refresh of the status:
# systemd kills the unit TimeoutStopSec after the stop, and a report cut off
# there leaves the issue marked tehas:running.
#
# The cycle's outcome is the result of pi-rukas's headless runner, which tehas
# work leaves in state/cycles/<issue>.result.json (tehas-cycle runner reads it):
# its status, the step it stopped in, its cap, branch, token usage and the
# pi-rukas commit it ran (buildCommit). Nothing is read from the tree the
# container wrote. The per-role usage, the reason of the last cap and the
# failure's kind come from state/cycles/<issue>.progress.json, which tehas work
# makes from the result, or from the runner's progress lines for an image whose
# result does not carry them.
#
# A result file that holds nothing is no result: the runner died before it
# could write one. Such a cycle is still reported, from what the host has of
# its own: the step its run log says it reached, the word tehas work left for
# how the container ended, and GitHub's answer for the bot's pull request of
# its branch. What each part means, and what is said from it, is the manual's
# (host/docs/tools.md, the section of tehasbot cycle on a runner that died).
#
# state/cycles/<issue>.runner_exit, which tehas work writes, gives the runner's
# own status for the comment: this command's own is 2 for a runner that died
# however it died. tehas work and tehasbot cycle each clear the files of this
# protocol before they start, so a cycle never reports an earlier one's end.
#
# A runner that exits 137 with no result had its container killed: the status
# reported is "killed" and the issue is parked. tehas work leaves in state/cycles/<issue>.oom how many
# processes in the container were killed for memory. The file is read here as
# digits and removed, the number is said in the comment when it is above 0,
# and it goes into the record for the improvement loop. Nothing is read from
# the runner's output. state/cycles/<issue>.forks, how many starts of a process
# the container's pids limit refused, is read the same way and said in the
# comment: after the first one the cycle's own commands may not have run.
#
# tehas work stops a cycle none of whose logs changed for TEHAS_WORK_STALL
# seconds and leaves the seconds in state/cycles/<issue>.stalled. The status
# reported is then "stalled", the issue is parked and the comment says how long
# nothing moved and at which step. A runner that did not stop at its own time
# limit is stopped by tehas work, which leaves the limit's seconds in
# state/cycles/<issue>.timed: the status is then "timeout", said the same way.
# The stop files name the step in flight, used where the result names none.
# Such a stop is on record as tehas's own cap,
# wall-clock or stall, with capReason naming the step and any cap the
# runner had named, unless the result names an end the runner reached first.
# So is a runner that stopped itself at the time limit and named no cap. Both
# go on record with tehas's own cap wall-clock and the step the cycle was in.
#
# A container that wrote no result and not one line of its stream never reached
# the driver's first event. tehas work leaves state/cycles/<issue>.nostart with
# the container's own exit status for that, and this command reports the cycle
# under tehas's own cap runner-never-started, with capReason naming that status
# and that no event was seen, and a step of no-step rather than one read from a
# log. Its run log is still opened for a branch, but no step is taken from it.
#
# A runner that exits 78 and leaves state/cycles/<issue>.lean-refused started no
# cycle either, and is parked as one that exits 4, with what to do next.
#
# A runner that exits 4 started no cycle: tehas work refused the start, over
# text that is not the text the dispatcher approved (its body or its title), a
# record it could not read, an issue it could not read, or an image or file the
# instance gives a cycle (TEHAS_WORK_IMAGES, TEHAS_WORK_FILES) that could not be
# given. Nothing ran, so the comment names no status, step, duration or usage as
# this cycle's: it names the exit status, who approved the start and when from
# the dispatcher's record, and where the log is. The record itself is removed
# with the report: an approval that started nothing is no approval a later start
# can rest on. The issue is parked, not reported as a cycle that stopped. The
# images of a cycle that ran are removed here when it is over: only the
# container read them. So is the cycle's own state directory, the copy of the
# code index, the memory and the sessions its agents wrote: no later cycle
# reads them.
#
# The result's "merged" counts only when the bot has a merged pull request for
# the issue on the cycle's branch; without one the issue is parked and nothing
# is removed. A pull request counts only when it names this issue: its head is
# feature/issue-<n>-..., as pi-rukas names a branch, or its body closes #<n>.
# A result or progress file that is a link is not read.
#
# A merged pull request for the issue ends the cycle as merged whatever the
# state says: a person may merge it while the cycle still reviews. The issue
# then only loses tehas:running, and tehasbot status labels it shipped. One a
# person closed without merging parks the issue without the retry boxes.
#
# A cycle a merge or a close interrupted keeps its workspace: what it had not
# pushed is nowhere else, and its comment names the directory. tehas removes a
# workspace only when the cycle ends with its change merged, or hands off with
# its pull request open for a human to merge.
#
# Only a run that ended as handoff (runner exit 10 or 11) with an open pull
# request, on the cap awaiting-human-merge, is reported as waiting for a merge,
# and only while no stable cap-hit of its progress ended the review on its
# round cap (reviewHeld) and the issue does not carry needs-human-attention as
# GitHub answers at the end. Any other handoff with a pull request is parked
# and its comment names the cap and the pull request.
#
# pi-rukas reports no handoff itself: tehas work gives a cycle
# PI_ENSEMBLE_HANDOFF_CHANNEL=host. The handoff the runner rendered is in its
# result, and goes into this command's one comment of the park (on the pull
# request, for a waiting merge), cleaned as an agent's words are, under
# pi-rukas's marker <!-- pi-rukas:handoff issue=<n> cap=<cap> -->.
#
# An issue that carries needs-human-attention when its cycle starts is run with
# --restart: the start is a person's answer to the handoff. pi-rukas then takes
# the label off. This command and the dispatcher remove it nowhere.
#
# The label change that ends a cycle, the question about the pull request and
# the call that asks what the number is are each tried three times. A pull
# request that could not be looked up is not taken for none: the issue is parked
# with a comment that says so and names the branch, which stays on the cycle's
# record. If GitHub still refuses it, or the cycle is killed outright, the issue
# stays marked tehas:running and the dispatcher's next poll finds it.
#
# The cap that ended a cycle and the failure before it go into the record. A
# cycle that parks the issue puts its park on the state store too (store park),
# before it says so on the issue: why it parked (provider-outage, else the cap,
# else the status), its step and its pull request. The store binds the park to
# the body the start was approved on, so the dispatcher holds a fresh
# tehas:ready while the issue still carries that text. A provider outage is
# said as one, and its park owes the issue one start on the dispatcher's own,
# unless this cycle was that start or was started by hand: the store derives
# that from the start on record. An outage is a result whose failure kind is
# "provider".
#
# A cycle that stopped without a pull request asks what to do next, in a comment
# with boxes. One that ended at child-timeout:develop first leaves a note of the
# attempt in the issue's body, which the next cycle reads, or, when that note is
# there and the text has not changed, offers no retry box and names tehas:plan
# (tehas-cycle last-attempt). The id of that comment goes on record as the
# park's question (store question-asked), with the sha256 of the body posted:
# the dispatcher takes a tick only in the question on record. A park the store
# would not take asks no question: its comment has no boxes. A question whose
# label could not be added is taken off the record again.
#
# A refresh (approval how "refresh") put tehas:refreshing on its pull request;
# it comes off once the cycle is reported, whatever the outcome. The pull request's title is set then too (console pr-title).
#
# The dispatcher stops a cycle whose work is done, and says why first in
# state/cycles/<issue>.done: closed (the issue was closed), merged, pr-closed
# (the bot's pull request for it) or quiet (the poll's quiet rule). A merged
# pull request ends the cycle as merged, as above. A cycle stopped because its
# issue was closed only loses tehas:running, and nothing is asked.
#
# When the cycle has been reported it leaves state/dispatch.again (for
# tehas-dispatch.path, after a poll already running) and starts one poll
# without waiting, so its slot is filled at once (tehas-cycle after). A failure
# is a warning: the timer still runs the poll.
#
# Once the comments and labels are done it ends the cycle's record on the
# state store (cycle-end): how it ended, the counts tehas work left, the
# branch, the pull request, the result and the progress, and one line of
# history. A store that does not take the end after five tries is an ERROR
# line naming every argument; the record stays open and the next start of the
# issue replaces it. A start that never ran (tehas work exit 4) takes its
# approval off the record with it: an approval that started nothing is no
# approval a later start can rest on.
#
# Then it records the run and its end in state/analytics.db, each call bounded,
# a failure a WARN line; TEHAS_ANALYTICS=off makes no call. With
# TEHAS_IMPROVE_REPO it leaves the cycle's record, with the image and the
# pi-rukas commit, in tmp/improve/window for the loop (tehas-cycle after).
#
# Instance settings (lib/config.sh): TEHAS_REPO, TEHAS_IMPROVE_REPO,
# TEHAS_ANALYTICS.
# From the environment: TEHAS_CYCLE_FROM=unit, which tehas-cycle@.service sets.
# tehas work is run with TEHAS_CYCLE_ATTEMPT, the attempt of this start.
# For tests: TEHAS_WORK_CMD, TEHAS_STATUS_CMD, TEHAS_CONSOLE_CMD,
# TEHAS_CYCLE_RETRY_WAIT (seconds between two tries of a GitHub call [5]) and
# TEHAS_CYCLE_STOP_GH_WAIT (seconds a GitHub call may take after a stop [20]).
set -uo pipefail

issue=${1:-}
if [ "$#" != 1 ] || ! [[ $issue =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]]; then
  echo "usage: tehasbot cycle <issue>" >&2
  exit 64
fi

here=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)
# shellcheck source=lib/config.sh
. "$here/../lib/config.sh"
# shellcheck source=lib/cycles.sh
. "$here/../lib/cycles.sh"
# shellcheck source=lib/labels.sh
. "$here/../lib/labels.sh"
# shellcheck source=lib/millwright.sh
. "$here/../lib/millwright.sh"
# shellcheck source=lib/store.sh
. "$here/../lib/store.sh"
tehas_load || exit 78
# shellcheck source=lib/questions.sh
. "$here/../lib/questions.sh"
tehas_require TEHAS_REPO

stop_gh_wait=${TEHAS_CYCLE_STOP_GH_WAIT:-20}
[[ $stop_gh_wait =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,3}$ ]] || stop_gh_wait=20

log() { TZ=UTC0 printf '%(%FT%TZ)T %s\n' -1 "$*"; }

if ! tehas_store_ready; then
  log "ERROR: the state store is not installed (run install.sh)"
  exit 78
fi

repo=$TEHAS_REPO
# The cycle's workspace on the host; tehas work makes it under this name.
workdir=$TEHAS_WORK_DIR-$issue
work=${TEHAS_WORK_CMD:-$here/work}
status_cmd=${TEHAS_STATUS_CMD:-$here/status}
console=${TEHAS_CONSOLE_CMD:-$here/console}
# A pinned cycle runs its own copy.
tc=${TEHAS_CODE:-$TEHAS_HOME}/libexec/tehas-cycle
cycles=$TEHAS_HOME/state/cycles

mkdir -p "$cycles" "$TEHAS_HOME/logs"
# The runner and the docker client it starts inherit this lock. If this
# process is killed outright the place stays taken until they are gone too.
exec 9>"$cycles/$issue.lock"
flock -w 5 9 || { log "a cycle for #$issue is already running"; exit 3; }
# The place is claimed with the lock, and the start goes on record under it.
# The new attempt is what the dispatcher waits for to know its unit came up,
# and a cycle that goes on to refuse its number still came up. From the unit
# the start uses the approval the dispatcher recorded; a run by hand is on
# record as one. A unit is never taken for a run by hand: a start the store
# refuses runs nothing.
rukas=$(tehas_rukas_commit)
[[ $rukas =~ ^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$ ]] || rukas=""
start_args=("$issue")
[ -z "$rukas" ] || start_args+=(--rukas "$rukas")
if [ "${TEHAS_CYCLE_FROM:-}" != unit ] && [ -z "${INVOCATION_ID:-}" ]; then
  start_args+=(--by-hand)
fi
# A busy store committed nothing, so the start is asked again. A store that
# timed out may have committed it: a start on record since this one began,
# under this lock, is its own. It is ended as refused and nothing runs.
start_t0=$EPOCHSECONDS
for try in 1 2 3; do
  tehas_store cycle-start "${start_args[@]}"
  start_rc=$?
  [ "$start_rc" = 1 ] || break
  case $tehas_store_word in
    busy) ;;
    timeout)
      start_said=$tehas_store_line
      if ! tehas_store_try 3 cycle-show "$issue"; then
        log "refuse #$issue: the state store did not answer the start in time, and whether it took it is not known ($tehas_store_word): $start_said"
        exit 75
      fi
      took=$(tehas_store_get ".cycle | select(. != null and .ended_at == null and .started_at >= $start_t0) | .attempt")
      if [[ $took =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,8}$ ]]; then
        tehas_store_try 5 cycle-end "$issue" --attempt "$took" --refused ||
          log "ERROR: the start of #$issue the store took late is not ended ($tehas_store_word): cycle-end $issue --attempt $took --refused"
        log "refuse #$issue: the state store took the start only after it timed out: $start_said"
        exit 75
      fi
      tehas_store_said 1 timeout "$start_said"
      ;;
    *) break ;;
  esac
  [ "$try" = 3 ] || sleep 1
done
case $start_rc in
  0) ;;
  1)
    case $tehas_store_word in
      busy | timeout)
        log "refuse #$issue: the state store did not take the start ($tehas_store_word): $tehas_store_line"
        exit 75
        ;;
    esac
    log "refuse #$issue: the state store refused the start: $tehas_store_line"
    exit 77
    ;;
  *)
    log "ERROR: #$issue: the state store cannot be used: $tehas_store_line"
    exit 78
    ;;
esac
# The start's fields in one read: one each, ended by a NUL, as $(jq -r) leaves it.
one='def v: tostring | gsub("\u0000"; "") | sub("\n+\\z"; "") + "\u0000";
  def s: if type == "string" then . else "" end; def n: if type == "number" then . else "" end;'
mapfile -d '' -t got < <(jq -j "$one"'(.attempt | v), (.started_by | s | v), (.how | s | v),
  (.approval_at | n | v), (if .requeued == true then 1 else 0 end | v)' <<<"$tehas_store_out" 2>/dev/null)
attempt=${got[0]-} start_by=${got[1]-} start_how=${got[2]-} approval_at=${got[3]-} requeued=${got[4]-}
if ! [[ $attempt =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,8}$ ]]; then
  log "ERROR: #$issue: the state store answered the start with no attempt"
  exit 78
fi
# end_refused: a start that refuses its number ends its record, so the next
# start of the issue is not one that replaces an open row.
end_refused() {
  tehas_store_try 5 cycle-end "$issue" --attempt "$attempt" --refused ||
    log "ERROR: the refused start of #$issue is not on record ($tehas_store_word): cycle-end $issue --attempt $attempt --refused"
}
retry_wait=${TEHAS_CYCLE_RETRY_WAIT:-5}
# Open issues only. GitHub is asked what the number is before a label is
# changed, a comment is posted or a container starts. The dispatcher has often
# marked the issue tehas:running by now, so a call that only failed is tried
# again rather than refusing a cycle that was already authorized. A transport
# that never answers is a refusal too, said as itself.
asked="" answer=""
for try in 1 2 3; do
  if answer=$(gh api "repos/$repo/issues/$issue" 2>/dev/null); then asked=1; break; fi
  answer=""
  [ "$try" = 3 ] || sleep "$retry_wait"
done
if [ -z "$asked" ]; then
  log "refuse #$issue: GitHub could not be asked what the number is"
  end_refused
  exit 65
fi
tehas_work_kind "$answer" || {
  case "$tehas_work_why" in
    pr) log "refuse #$issue: it is a pull request, not an issue the factory works on" ;;
    closed) log "refuse #$issue: the issue is closed" ;;
    *) log "refuse #$issue: GitHub's answer for it could not be read" ;;
  esac
  end_refused
  exit 65
}

# A cycle stopped by a signal has until systemd's TimeoutStopSec to report:
# then every call to GitHub is tried once and bounded.
signalled=0 tries=3
shadow=""
gh_call() {
  [ -z "$shadow" ] || printf '%s\0' "$@" $'\x1e' >>"$shadow.gh"
  if [ "$signalled" = 1 ] || [ -n "${gh_wait:-}" ]; then
    # KILL: the report ignores TERM, and gh inherits that.
    timeout -s KILL "${gh_wait:-$stop_gh_wait}" gh "$@"
  else
    gh "$@"
  fi
}
gh_tried() {
  local warn=$1 try
  shift
  for ((try = 1; try <= tries; try++)); do
    gh_call "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
    [ "$try" = "$tries" ] || sleep "$retry_wait"
  done
  log "$warn"
  return 1
}
comment() { gh_tried "WARN: comment on #$issue failed" issue comment "$issue" --repo "$repo" --body "$1"; }
relabel() { gh_tried "WARN: label change on #$issue failed: $*" issue edit "$issue" --repo "$repo" "$@"; }
# gh_label_off <path>: take one label off a pull request through the REST
# issues endpoint, not `gh pr edit`: that asks a GraphQL question the bot's token
# may not answer (issue #120). A 404 says the label is not there, as wanted.
gh_label_off() {
  local out
  out=$(gh_call api -X DELETE "$1" 2>&1) && return 0
  [[ $out == *"Not Found"* ]]
}
# attention_off: the issue does not carry needs-human-attention now, as GitHub
# answers. No answer counts as carrying it: a pull request is said to wait only
# for a merge only when that is known.
attention_off() {
  local try json
  for ((try = 1; try <= tries; try++)); do
    if json=$(gh_call api "repos/$repo/issues/$issue" 2>/dev/null) && jq -e 'type == "object"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$json"; then
      jq -e '(.labels // []) | any(.name? == "needs-human-attention") | not' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$json"
      return
    fi
    [ "$try" = "$tries" ] || sleep "$retry_wait"
  done
  log "WARN: the labels of #$issue could not be read, so its pull request is not said to wait only for a merge"
  return 1
}
# post_question <question.json>: post the park question. Its id is left in
# $posted, the sha256 of the body posted in $posted_sum. Returns 1 when it
# could not be posted.
posted=""
posted_sum=""
post_question() {
  local body
  body=$(q_render park "$issue" 1 "$1") || return 1
  posted_sum=$(printf '%s' "$body" | sha256sum | cut -c1-64)
  posted=$(gh_call api "repos/$repo/issues/$issue/comments" -f body="$body" --jq .id 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [[ $posted =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,15}$ ]]
}
# Removes the workspace of a cycle that ended with its change merged, or that
# handed off with its pull request open for a human to merge, and with it the
# one tehas work moved aside when this issue was retried. A cycle stopped by a
# merge or a close someone else made is not tidied: its work may exist nowhere
# else. Only a directory under one that tehas work marked is removed.
tidy() {
  if [ -f "${workdir%/*}/.tehas-workspaces" ]; then
    rm -rf "$workdir" "$workdir.before-retry" ||
      log "WARN: could not remove the workspace $workdir"
  else
    log "WARN: ${workdir%/*} is not marked by tehas work, leaving the workspace $workdir"
  fi
}

started=$EPOCHSECONDS
# An issue a handoff left with needs-human-attention starts again only on a
# person's answer: the label or the tick that started this cycle, or the one
# start after a provider outage the dispatcher makes for the account that
# authorised the cycle before. That answer is what --restart tells the runner.
# It lets the start past pi-rukas's attention gate and takes the label off the
# issue itself: nothing here removes it.
restart=""
if jq -e '(.labels // []) | any(.name? == "needs-human-attention")' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$answer"; then
  restart=--restart
fi
# A count of kills still here is an earlier cycle's, one that was killed
# outright before it could report. The one start the dispatcher makes on its
# own after a provider outage is on record as requeued: that start's cycle, if
# an outage parks it again, waits for a person.
rm -f "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.oom" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.forks" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.stalled" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.timed" \
  "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.stopped" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.stopped.new" \
  "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.result.json" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.progress.json" \
  "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.nostart" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.lean-refused" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.done" \
  "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.runner_exit" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.status"
# The trap is set before the runner starts, so no signal falls between.
wpid=""
stopped=0
trap 'stopped=1 signalled=1; [ -z "$wpid" ] || kill -TERM "$wpid" 2>/dev/null' TERM INT HUP
TEHAS_CYCLE_ATTEMPT=$attempt "$work" "$issue" ${restart:+"$restart"} >>"$TEHAS_HOME/logs/dispatch-work-$issue.log" 2>&1 &
wpid=$!
[ "$stopped" = 0 ] || kill -TERM "$wpid" 2>/dev/null
wait "$wpid"
rc=$?
# A signal ends the wait early, with a status that is not the runner's. The
# runner is still stopping its container: wait again for its own status. A
# wait that answers 127 found the runner already reaped, so rc stands.
while [ "$stopped" = 1 ]; do
  stopped=0
  wait "$wpid"
  r=$?
  [ "$r" = 127 ] || rc=$r
done
# The report is made even if a stop arrives now: leaving tehas:running on the
# issue would park it for good.
trap '' TERM INT HUP
[ "$signalled" = 0 ] || tries=1
# The report in Rust, where the instance asks for it: the signals stay ignored
# across the exec, and the lock stays held. Without the program bash reports.
[ "${TEHAS_CYCLE_REPORT:-}" != rust ] || [ ! -x "$tc" ] || exec "$tc" report "$issue" --attempt "$attempt" --rc "$rc" --started "$started" --signalled "$signalled" --by "$start_by" --how "$start_how" --approval-at "$approval_at" --requeued "$requeued"
# The report's shadow (#256): tehas-cycle decides beside this report, from the
# same files, while state/cycle-shadow.on is there. It never fails the report.
ended=$EPOCHSECONDS mins=$(((ended - started) / 60))
[ ! -e "$TEHAS_HOME/state/cycle-shadow.on" ] || ! timeout 60 "$tc" shadow "$issue" --attempt "$attempt" --rc "$rc" --started "$started" --signalled "$signalled" --by "$start_by" --how "$start_how" --approval-at "$approval_at" --requeued "$requeued" --now "$ended" >/dev/null 2>&1 || shadow=$cycles/shadow-$issue
[ ! -f "${workdir%/*}/.tehas-workspaces" ] || rm -rf "$workdir.images" ||
  log "WARN: could not remove the images $workdir.images"
# The cycle's own cache, memory and sessions, made by tehas work beside the
# workspace. A cycle that stopped without a pull request keeps its workspace
# for a person to read, and not what its agents wrote in their own directories.
[ ! -f "${workdir%/*}/.tehas-workspaces" ] || rm -rf "$workdir.state" ||
  log "WARN: could not remove the cycle's state $workdir.state"

# The runner's result and the summary of its progress, as tehas work wrote
# them. Not through a link: these are host files, and a link would be
# something else.
result=$cycles/$issue.result.json
progress=$cycles/$issue.progress.json
if [ -L "$result" ]; then
  log "WARN: the result of #$issue is a link, it is not read"
  result=/dev/null
fi
[ ! -L "$progress" ] || progress=/dev/null
# A runner that died halfway wrote no result at all, leaving this file empty.
# An empty file is no result, and says nothing of its own, so the log is read
# instead (below): nothing of a cycle that did write a result is taken from a
# log, where the two could disagree. A file that is not empty and not this
# schema is a result all the same, and still none, and said as one.
no_result=0
if [ ! -s "$result" ]; then
  result=/dev/null
  no_result=1
fi
# Said once a cycle, not twice: the comment of the park names it too, and a
# reader of the poll's log wants the one line that says what could not be read.
[ "$no_result" = 0 ] ||
  log "WARN: the result of #$issue holds nothing, so its step is read from its run log"
outcome=$("$TEHAS_HOME/libexec/tehas-cycle" runner outcome "$result" "$issue") || outcome='{}'
# The result's fields in one read, kept apart until the steps below use them.
# The handoff is this command's to post (tehas work gives a cycle
# PI_ENSEMBLE_HANDOFF_CHANNEL=host); it loses pi-rukas's marker line, which goes
# back on top of the comment. The cap that ended the cycle is from the result's
# published vocabulary; it and parkReason are reduced and cut short.
mapfile -d '' -t got < <(jq -j "$one"'def w: tostring | gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]"; "");
  (.status // "unknown" | w | v), ([.failedStep, .lastStep] | map(strings | select(. != "")) | first // "-" | w | v),
  (.branch // "" | v), (.buildCommit // "" | v),
  ((try (.handoffBody // "" | sub("^<!-- pi-rukas:handoff [^\n]*-->\n*"; "")) catch "") | v),
  (.parkReason // "" | tostring | gsub("[^a-z-]"; "") | .[0:40] | v),
  (.cap // "" | tostring | gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9_.:-]"; "") | .[0:80] | v)' <<<"$outcome" 2>/dev/null)
status=${got[0]-} step=${got[1]-} result_branch=${got[2]-} build=${got[3]-} handoff=${got[4]-}
park_reason=${got[5]-} cap=${got[6]-}
status=${status:-unknown}
step=${step:--}
# A cycle that wrote no result has no word of its own for how it ended. The
# word of tehas work, the one that watched the container, stands in: it is the
# only account of the end, and it names what it saw rather than what the report
# would prefer. `killed` is the only word tehas work writes to that file.
work_status=""
if [ "$no_result" = 1 ]; then
  [ ! -f "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.status" ] ||
    [ -L "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.status" ] ||
    read -r work_status <"${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.status" 2>/dev/null
  case "$work_status" in killed) status=killed ;; esac
fi
# The files tehas work leaves for a cycle it watched, and what each proves, are
# written in lib/cycles.sh. The two of the runner's end are there when the
# container was started and given its time, and both are absent when tehas
# work stopped before that, in which case nothing of this issue's earlier runs
# is read for this one.
runner_exit_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.runner_exit
watched=0
if [ -f "$runner_exit_file" ] && [ ! -L "$runner_exit_file" ]; then
  watched=1
fi
progress_seen=0
[ -e "$progress" ] && [ ! -L "$progress" ] && progress_seen=1
# The note tehas work leaves for the container it watched to its end that wrote
# no result and not one line of its stream: the container's own exit status, on
# a line of its own. Digits, read as every other file of the cycle's is: anything
# else is no note, and a cycle whose note reads as no note is reported the way it
# was before the note existed. The file's presence is the marker that says this
# cycle started no runner, as the presence of the runner's exit file says the run
# was watched; the digits only fill in the words of the comment. A note whose
# status is 0 is no note: 0 is the end of a container that finished cleanly.
nostart_seen=0
nostart_rc=""
# The word this command puts on record for a cycle whose container never came
# up. Not a step of the runner's vocabulary: no step of pi-rukas is named here,
# and the word is kept clear of the three the analytics reader reads a planning
# turn's rc for (`interrupted`, `not-started`, `not-readable`), so a cycle that
# never began is never mistaken for a start that was refused at exit 4.
nostart_step=no-step
nostart_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.nostart
[ -f "$nostart_file" ] && [ ! -L "$nostart_file" ] || nostart_file=
if [ -n "$nostart_file" ]; then
  LC_ALL=C read -r -n 100 nostart_rc _ 2>/dev/null <"$nostart_file"
  [[ $nostart_rc =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,2}$ ]] || nostart_rc=""
fi
[ -n "$nostart_rc" ] && nostart_seen=1
rm -f "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.nostart"
# A step is read from the run log only where the cycle wrote no result at all:
# there the runner died before it could name one, and the log's own
# step-started lines are all there is. Where a result was written, that result
# names the step and nothing of a log is read for it, so the two cannot
# disagree. Only a cycle tehas work watched has a log of its own, and the
# summary it wrote of that log is what says so: only such a cycle is read for
# one. The summary is this cycle's own as well: the cycle removed it when it
# took its lock, before tehas work started.
read_log_steps=$no_result
[ "$watched" = 1 ] && [ "$progress_seen" = 1 ] || read_log_steps=0
# Where the step now on record was read is said below, beside the words the
# comment uses for it.
# A step of tehas work's own files: the step it saw in flight when it stopped
# the container at the stall or the time limit. Read here, before the log is
# opened, because the two files answer the question the log is read for: where
# one of them names a step, the run log is not read at all, so a step of a log
# cannot stand in for a step tehas work itself saw. The two files are read for
# their seconds below, and for the step here, from the same lines.
stall_step=""
stall_secs=""
stall_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.stalled
if [ -f "$stall_file" ] && [ ! -L "$stall_file" ]; then
  { read -r stall_secs; read -r stall_step; } < <(head -c 100 "$stall_file" 2>/dev/null)
  stall_step=$(tr -cd 'A-Za-z0-9_.-' <<<"${stall_step:-}" | head -c 40)
  [ "$stall_step" != - ] || stall_step=""
fi
timed_step_file=""
timed_secs=""
timed_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.timed
if [ -f "$timed_file" ] && [ ! -L "$timed_file" ]; then
  # Its one line: the limit, who stopped the cycle, the step it was in.
  LC_ALL=C read -r -n 200 timed_secs _ timed_step_file _ 2>/dev/null <"$timed_file"
  [[ $timed_step_file =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{1,40}$ ]] && [ "$timed_step_file" != - ] ||
    timed_step_file=""
fi
# Whether a step is still wanted from the run log: the result names none, and
# neither of tehas work's two files names one either. The files hold their step
# only with a stop of their own: the seconds of the stall, the limit of the
# time. Without those the step they name is the last verdict of a step, not what
# ended the cycle, and it goes in the reason rather than on record. Their own
# words are read once here, and the seconds go with them, so the report of the
# stop below and this answer cannot disagree about what the files say.
step_wanted=1
[ "$step" = - ] || step_wanted=0
# A cycle the note says never started has no step to read for: its log held no
# line. Only the step goes: the branch of a nostart cycle is still asked about
# as for any other cycle that wrote no result, so the one pass that names it
# stays open. The word is the same one tehas work's stop files use for the same
# answer, and it sits with them because a nostart cycle whose stop files name no
# step is the only kind that reaches the pass at all.
[ -z "$nostart_rc" ] || step_wanted=0
[[ $stall_secs =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,8})$ ]] || stall_step=""
[[ $timed_secs =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,8})$ ]] || timed_step_file=""
[ -z "$stall_step" ] && [ -z "$timed_step_file" ] || step_wanted=0
# The status of the runner itself, as tehas work saw it (libexec/work). This
# command's own status is 2 for a runner that died however it died, so the
# comment would otherwise say 2 for a runner that panicked with 134, and 137
# only for a container tehas work saw killed. Where tehas work wrote the file
# of the runner's own status, that is the word to name; with none, the status
# this command's own wait saw stands.
if [ "$watched" = 1 ]; then
  # The file is read, then the word it holds is checked: a read that finds no
  # line at all (a truncated or empty write by tehas work) ends with the
  # variable empty and answers nothing, so the check is made after it and not
  # on it.
  read -r runner_rc <"$runner_exit_file" 2>/dev/null
  runner_rc=${runner_rc:-}
  # The shape every other state file of tehas work is read with, and
  # normalised the same way: the comment names a number, not the bytes of one.
  [[ $runner_rc =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,2})$ ]] || runner_rc=$rc
  runner_rc=$((10#$runner_rc))
else
  runner_rc=$rc
fi
# Both words of the run are read once: they are of this cycle, and a cycle that
# is killed outright leaves them for the next one of the issue to report as its
# own. The cycle's own start clears them too; this is the other half.
rm -f "$runner_exit_file" "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.status"
# A runner that died wrote no result, so neither the step it was in nor the
# branch commit-pr pushed is known. The log it left is: its stable
# step-started lines name the steps it reached. GitHub decides whether a pull
# request exists; these names only say which branch to ask about, so no line of
# a log can make this report a pull request there is none of. Only a cycle tehas
# work watched has a log of its own. It is read for the branch, which the host
# never names, and for the step when nothing else named it. A branch of a log
# goes with the step of the same run, so both come of the one pass.
ran_log=""
if [ "$read_log_steps" = 1 ]; then
  # The newest by its own name: tehas work names the file for the UTC time of
  # its start, so the newest cycle of this issue has the last name, and the
  # names of a directory sort in that order by themselves. Nothing read out of
  # the file decides which log is the one to read, and no other log of
  # the issue is read at all when none is there: this command writes no log.
  for ran in "$TEHAS_HOME/logs/work-$issue"-*.jsonl; do
    [ -f "$ran" ] && [ ! -L "$ran" ] || continue
    [ -z "$ran_log" ] && ran_log=$ran
    [[ $ran > $ran_log ]] && ran_log=$ran
  done
fi
log_step=""
log_branch=""
log_read=""
log_words=0
log_from=result
if [ -n "$ran_log" ]; then
  # One pass over the log gives both words: the last step the runner says it
  # started, and the step right after its last commit-pr. pi-rukas names the
  # branch it pushed as the step of the push, so that one name, and no other
  # step of the log, is this issue's branch. A step of any other shape, a run
  # whose steps stop at commit-pr, and a run that never got there all leave it
  # empty, so GitHub is asked about no branch of its own. tehas work writes no
  # branch of its own, so nothing else says it for a cycle that wrote no result.
  { read -r log_step; read -r log_branch; read -r log_read; } < <("$TEHAS_HOME/libexec/tehas-cycle" runner run "$ran_log" "$issue")
  # A log the helper answered nothing at all for (no file, no number for the
  # issue) is no log whose words were read, and says nothing of the cycle. The
  # third line is the only place its answer says that, and the one word the
  # comment can name it by: `read from its run log` is said only of a log the
  # helper read whole or from its end, never of one it could not open.
  case "$log_read" in
    whole | truncated) log_words=1 ;;
    *)
      log "WARN: the run log $ran_log of #$issue could not be read, so its step and its branch are not known"
      ;;
  esac
  # The log is read for the step only where nothing else names one, so its word
  # is taken only then: a step of tehas work's own file has the last word, and a
  # log opened for the branch alone does not say the step of the cycle. The
  # words the helper gave are kept either way, so the comment can name the log
  # as the source of a step that was taken, and a log it could not open at all
  # is no source of anything.
  if [ "$log_words" = 1 ] && [ "$step_wanted" = 1 ] && [ -n "$log_step" ]; then
    step=$log_step
    log_from=log
  elif [ "$log_words" != 1 ]; then
    log_branch=""
  fi
fi
step=${step:--}
# Where the step now on record was read: `result` named it; `log` is a
# step-started line of the runner itself, said only of a step the block above
# took from a log the helper opened and read whole or from its end. The two
# other words, `stalled` and `timed`, name a step tehas work itself saw in
# flight when it stopped the container, and are set where its files are read,
# below: a step of a host file is never said as the runner's word from its log,
# and a result that named the step keeps `result`, which is said in no wording
# of its own.
step_from=$log_from
[ "$step" != - ] || step_from=result
# No step of a cycle whose container never started: tehas work's note is what
# says so, and it is a word of the one command that watched the container, not a
# name read out of a log that held nothing. Held to a cycle that wrote no result
# at all, as the note is: a result that names an end has the last word, and the
# step of a result is never replaced.
[ -z "$nostart_rc" ] || [ "$no_result" = 0 ] || { step=$nostart_step; step_from=nostart; }
step=${step:--}
# The step of tehas work's stall file stands in where the result and the log
# name none, as the one its timed file names does: each was written by tehas
# work, which watched this cycle, so neither is a name read out of a log. Held
# to a stop of its own: the seconds must be there, as the report of the stop
# below makes the same test.
[ "$step" != - ] || [ -z "$stall_step" ] || { step=$stall_step; step_from=stalled; }
# The runner says 137 only for a container that was killed under it with no
# result written. A result that names an end has the last word.
# The word of tehas work for a container it watched has the last word already; this
# is for a cycle whose runner was killed under tehas work itself, with no file
# of the runner's status behind it.
if [ "$rc" = 137 ] && [ "$watched" = 0 ]; then
  case "$status" in running | unknown) status=killed ;; esac
fi
# How many processes in the container were killed for memory, as tehas work
# counted them. One short line of digits, or nothing.
kills=0
oom_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.oom
if [ -f "$oom_file" ] && [ ! -L "$oom_file" ]; then
  IFS= LC_ALL=C read -r -n 16 kills 2>/dev/null <"$oom_file"
  [[ $kills =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,8})$ ]] || kills=0
fi
rm -f "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.oom"
killed=""
[ "$kills" = 0 ] || killed=" $kills processes were killed for memory in its container."
forks=0
forks_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.forks
if [ -f "$forks_file" ] && [ ! -L "$forks_file" ]; then
  IFS= LC_ALL=C read -r -n 16 forks 2>/dev/null <"$forks_file"
  [[ $forks =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,8})$ ]] || forks=0
fi
rm -f "${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.forks"
# The stall file of tehas work: the seconds, and on a second line the step
# that was in flight, which stands in for a step the result does not name.
stop_secs=0 stop_step=""
stop_read() { # <file>: set stop_secs and, when it names one, stop_step
  local at=""
  stop_secs=0
  [ -f "$1" ] && [ ! -L "$1" ] || return 0
  { read -r stop_secs; read -r at; } < <(head -c 100 "$1" 2>/dev/null)
  [[ $stop_secs =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,8})$ ]] || stop_secs=0
  at=$(tr -cd 'A-Za-z0-9_.-' <<<"$at" | head -c 40)
  [ "$stop_secs" = 0 ] || [ -z "$at" ] || [ "$at" = - ] || stop_step=$at
}
stall_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.stalled
stop_read "$stall_file"
stalled=$stop_secs
rm -f "$stall_file"
# The step of the stall file stands in where the result and the log name none:
# it was written by tehas work, which watched this cycle, so it is tehas work's
# own word for what it saw in flight.
[ "$step" != - ] || [ -z "$stop_step" ] || { step=$stop_step; step_from=stalled; }
# The image the container started from, as tehas work put it on this attempt's
# record, its pi-rukas label and when that id was built. None without a record.
image_id="" image_rukas="" image_built=""
if tehas_store cycle-show "$issue"; then
  read -r image_id image_rukas < <(jq -r --argjson a "$attempt" \
    '.cycle | select(. != null and .attempt == $a and .image != null) | "\(.image) \(.rukas_commit // "")"' \
    <<<"$tehas_store_out" 2>/dev/null)
  [[ $image_id =~ ^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]] || image_id=""
  [[ $image_rukas =~ ^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$ ]] || image_rukas=""
  [ -z "$image_id" ] || image_built=$(timeout -k 5 30 docker image inspect --format '{{.Created}}' "$image_id" 2>/dev/null)
else
  log "WARN: the record of #$issue could not be read for its image ($tehas_store_word)"
fi
if [ "$stalled" != 0 ]; then
  case "$status" in running | unknown | interrupted) status=stalled ;; esac
  killed="$killed None of its logs changed for $((stalled / 60)) min at step \`$step\`, so tehas stopped it before its time limit."
fi
# The cycle ended at its time limit: tehas work stopped a runner that did not
# stop at it, or the runner stopped itself there. The file says the limit, who
# stopped it and the step it was in, for a result that names none.
timed=0 timed_by="" timed_step=""
timed_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.timed
if [ -f "$timed_file" ] && [ ! -L "$timed_file" ]; then
  LC_ALL=C read -r -n 200 timed timed_by timed_step _ 2>/dev/null <"$timed_file"
  [[ $timed =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,8})$ ]] || timed=0
  [[ $timed_step =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{1,40}$ ]] && [ "$timed_step" != - ] || timed_step=""
fi
rm -f "$timed_file"
if [ "$timed" != 0 ]; then
  case "$status" in running | unknown | interrupted) status=timeout ;; esac
  # The step of tehas work's own file stands in for a step the result names
  # none, as the one the stall file names does: each was written by tehas work,
  # which watched this cycle, so neither is a name read out of a log.
  [ "$step" != - ] || [ -z "$timed_step" ] || { step=$timed_step; step_from=timed; }
  if [ "$timed_by" = runner ]; then
    killed="$killed It reached its time limit of $((timed / 60)) min at step \`$step\`, and the runner stopped there."
  else
    killed="$killed It ran past its time limit of $((timed / 60)) min at step \`$step\`, and the runner did not stop on its own, so tehas stopped it."
  fi
fi
# The marker tehas stop leaves: epoch seconds and an account, read as .timed is
# (plain file, at most 200 bytes, both fields checked) and removed either way.
# The status stays as it is.
stopped_by="" stopped_at="" stopped_at_shown="" stopped_by_person=0
stopped_file=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.stopped
if [ -f "$stopped_file" ] && [ ! -L "$stopped_file" ]; then
  LC_ALL=C read -r -n 200 stopped_at stopped_by _ 2>/dev/null <"$stopped_file"
  [[ $stopped_at =~ ^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,9})$ ]] || stopped_at=""
  [[ $stopped_by =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,64}$ ]] || stopped_by=""
fi
rm -f "$stopped_file"
if [ -n "$stopped_at" ]; then
  stopped_at_shown=$(date -u -d "@$stopped_at" +%FT%TZ 2>/dev/null) || stopped_at_shown=""
fi
if [ -n "$stopped_at_shown" ]; then
  stopped_by=$(printf '%s' "$stopped_by" | tr -cd 'A-Za-z0-9._-' | head -c 64)
  [ -n "$stopped_by" ] || stopped_by="name not recorded"
  stopped_by_person=1
  killed="$killed A person stopped it with \`tehas stop\` ($stopped_by, at $stopped_at_shown)."
fi
[ "$forks" = 0 ] || killed="$killed Its container reached its limit of processes and $forks starts of a process were refused: what the cycle ran after that may not have run."
branch=$result_branch
# Two arms, and they are not the same question. The first is the branch of this
# issue, as pi-rukas names it: the one grammar of tehas_branch_pattern, which
# the helper holds for the log's name and this command holds for the result's.
# The second is the container's own branch name, reduced to the characters
# GitHub can be asked about at all: a handoff that pushed to a branch of its own
# naming is said by that name, and a name of that shape is no one's issue's
# branch. A log's name never reaches the second arm: the helper already held it
# to the first.
branch_pattern=$(tehas_branch_pattern "$issue") || branch_pattern='^$'
[[ $branch =~ $branch_pattern ]] ||
  [[ $branch =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._/-]{0,79}$ ]] || branch=""
# No result to name the branch: the runner's own step names do, when it reached
# commit-pr. That branch is then the one GitHub is asked about, and the branch on
# record when the answer cannot be read.
[ -n "$branch" ] || branch=$log_branch
# The pi-rukas commit the runner says it ran, for the improvement loop's check
# of whether a fix was in it.
[[ $build =~ ^[0-9a-f]{40}$ ]] || build=""
# A stable cap-hit that ended the review on its round cap and sent the cycle on
# to ci: the pull request has review findings no round answered. The why of a
# cap: the failure tehas work read from the result.
# The progress, read once: each field ended by a NUL, one that is not text empty.
mapfile -d '' -t got < <(head -c 1000000 "$progress" 2>/dev/null | jq -n -j 'input
  | def kept($set; $most): if type == "string" then gsub("[^" + $set + "]"; "") | .[0:$most] else "" end;
  def text: if type == "string" then gsub("\u0000"; "") else "" end;
  (.reviewHeld == true | tostring), (.reason // "" | if type == "string" then gsub("\n"; " ") else . end
  | kept("A-Za-z0-9 _.,:;/()=+-"; 200)), (.from.failure // "" | text),
  (.failure.kind // "" | kept("a-z-"; 40)), (.failure.role // "" | kept("A-Za-z0-9_.-"; 40)),
  (.failure.cause // "" | kept("A-Za-z0-9_.:-"; 60)) | . + "\u0000"' 2>/dev/null)
review_held=${got[0]:-} reason=${got[1]:-} failure_from=${got[2]:-} failure_kind=${got[3]:-}
failure_role=${got[4]:-} failure_cause=${got[5]:-}
provider=0
[ "$failure_from" != result ] || [ "$failure_kind" != provider ] || provider=1
# When tehas stopped the cycle itself, the cap is tehas's own: the time limit
# or the stall, or a person's `tehas stop`. The runner's cap is kept only where
# its result names an end it reached first, such as a handoff; the cap of a
# runner stopped mid-step is the last verdict of a step, not why the cycle
# ended, and goes in the reason. So a stop tehas made says who made it, and at
# which step, wherever cap is used: the park record, the comment's marker, the
# history line and the improvement record.
own=""
if [ "$nostart_seen" = 1 ] && [ "$no_result" = 1 ]; then
  own=runner-never-started
  stop_why="the container ended with the exit status $nostart_rc and wrote no line of its stream, so the driver wrote no event and no step started"
elif [ "$timed" != 0 ] && { [ -z "$cap" ] || [ "$status" = timeout ]; }; then
  own=wall-clock
  if [ "$timed_by" = runner ]; then
    stop_why="the cycle reached its time limit, TEHAS_WORK_TIMEOUT=${timed}s, at step $step"
  else
    stop_why="tehas work stopped the container at the time limit of ${timed}s"
  fi
elif [ "$stalled" != 0 ] && { [ -z "$cap" ] || [ "$status" = stalled ]; }; then
  own=stall
  stop_why="tehas work stopped the container when no log changed for ${stalled}s"
elif [ "$stopped_by_person" = 1 ] && [ -z "$cap" ]; then
  # No cap of tehas's own: the stop is named in the reason only.
  reason=$(printf '%s' "tehas stop ended the cycle, run by $stopped_by at $stopped_at_shown, step $step in flight${reason:+; $reason}" | head -c 200)
fi
if [ -n "$own" ]; then
  [ "$own" = wall-clock ] && [ "$timed_by" = runner ] ||
    [ "$own" = runner-never-started ] || stop_why="$stop_why, step $step in flight"
  [ -z "$cap" ] || stop_why="$stop_why; the runner had named the cap $cap"
  reason=$(printf '%s' "$stop_why${reason:+; $reason}" | head -c 200)
  cap=$own
fi
# The token usage: a row per role from the progress, and the totals, which
# count failed dispatches too. Numbers only: anything else counts as nothing,
# on either line. The two lines are read from the two files of the cycle and
# can disagree, which is what the zero below is for: where the result names no
# dispatches at all the rows are the progress' own and the totals are the same rows
# added up, so the table does not close with a row of zeros under rows that are
# not zeros. The one decision of which totals to print is made once, by
# `usage_totals`, and handed to the table as one JSON value: it answers the two
# questions the table needs together, whether any row of the progress tells of
# work at all, and what their sum is. A row is a record of numbers only when all
# five of its counts are numbers: one that holds a word, an object or a null is
# the word of a container, and where any row is such a one the totals stay the
# result's own, zeros and all. No sum is ever of anything the progress did not
# say, and no count of a row that could not be read is added to anything.

# usage_totals <rows.json>: one JSON object, the answer of the rows to both of
# the table's questions, read from standard input. `told` says what the rows
# are: true when a row records work, false when there is no row or one of them
# holds a word in place of a count, and "unreadable" when the stream is no
# array of them at all. `total` is the five counts added up, or null when no row
# tells of work. A progress that could not be parsed is told apart from one that
# holds no rows, and neither the one nor the other is summed into anything.
usage_totals() { # < rows.json
  jq -c '
    def n: if type == "number" and . >= 0 then . else 0 end;
    # A row tells of work only when all five of its counts are numbers: one
    # that holds a word, an object or a null is the word of a container.
    def told: [.dispatches, .turns, .input, .output, .cacheRead]
      | map(type) | all(.[]; . == "number");
    if type != "array" then {told: "unreadable", total: null}
    else ([.[] | objects]) as $r
    | if ($r | length) == 0 or ($r | map(told) | any(.[]; . == false))
      then {told: false, total: null}
      else {told: true,
            total: {dispatches: ([$r[].dispatches] | map(n) | add // 0),
                    turns: ([$r[].turns] | map(n) | add // 0),
                    input: ([$r[].input] | map(n) | add // 0),
                    output: ([$r[].output] | map(n) | add // 0),
                    cacheRead: ([$r[].cacheRead] | map(n) | add // 0)}}
      end
    end' 2>/dev/null
}

usage_total_own=$(jq -c '.usage // {}' <<<"$outcome" 2>/dev/null) || usage_total_own='{}'
[ -n "$usage_total_own" ] || usage_total_own='{}'
# Whether the result speaks of dispatches at all. The outcome always carries
# the five counts, with nothing in place of one the image never named, so a
# count that is no number is the word for a result that says nothing of work.
# A file jq cannot read is one that says nothing either: the rows still go up
# on their own, which is what the table can say with no totals of its own.
usage_total_said=$(jq -c '
  if type != "object" then false
  else [.dispatches, .turns, .input, .output, .cacheRead]
       | any(.[]; . != 0 and . != null) end
' <<<"$usage_total_own" 2>/dev/null) || usage_total_said=false
[ -n "$usage_total_said" ] || usage_total_said=false
# The one answer of the progress's rows, read once and passed whole: no shell
# value re-tests it, and the table below only asks which totals to print.
usage_rows=$(jq -c '[.roles[]? | objects]' "$progress" 2>/dev/null | usage_totals) ||
  usage_rows=""
[ -n "$usage_rows" ] || usage_rows='{"told":"unreadable","total":null}'
[ "$(jq -r '.told | tostring' <<<"$usage_rows" 2>/dev/null)" != unreadable ] ||
  log "WARN: the progress of #$issue could not be read, so its rows are not summed into the totals"
usage=$(head -c 1000000 "$progress" 2>/dev/null | jq -r \
  --argjson rows "$usage_rows" --argjson total "$usage_total_own" \
  --arg told "$usage_total_said" '
  def n: if type == "number" and . >= 0 then . else 0 end;
  [.roles[]? | objects] as $r
  | (if ($rows.total | type) == "object" and $told == "false" then $rows.total else $total end) as $t
  | if ($r | length) == 0 and ($t.dispatches | n) == 0 then "" else
      (["| Role | Dispatches | Turns | Input | Output | Cache read |", "|---|---|---|---|---|---|"]
       + ($r | map("| \(.role | tostring | gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]"; "")) | \(.dispatches | n) | \(.turns | n) | \(.input | n) | \(.output | n) | \(.cacheRead | n) |"))
       + ["| **total, all dispatches** | \($t.dispatches | n) | \($t.turns | n) | \($t.input | n) | \($t.output | n) | \($t.cacheRead | n) |"])
      | join("\n") end' 2>/dev/null || echo "")

# The pull requests of the bot on the cycle's branch, in any state. The state
# file's word "merged" is a claim: it counts only with a merged pull request
# to show for it. A person may also merge or close one while the cycle runs.
# The branch name comes from a file the
# cycle wrote, so only a pull request the bot opened counts, and only one that
# names this issue: its head is the issue's branch as pi-rukas names it,
# feature/issue-<n>-..., or its body closes #<n>. A branch of another issue
# finds nothing. Without an answer a handoff would be taken for a cycle that
# left nothing.
# A list GitHub would not give is not an empty one: $unread says so.
prs="[]"
unread=0
if [ -n "$branch" ]; then
  for ((try = 1; try <= tries; try++)); do
    if prs=$(gh_call pr list --repo "$repo" --head "$branch" --author "@me" --state all \
      --json url,state,body,headRefName --limit 20 2>/dev/null) && jq -e 'type == "array"' >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$prs"; then
      break
    fi
    prs="[]"
    if [ "$try" = "$tries" ]; then
      unread=1
      log "WARN: the pull requests of $(tr -cd 'A-Za-z0-9_./-' <<<"$branch") could not be read"
    else
      sleep "$retry_wait"
    fi
  done
fi
# Whether a pull request names this issue, as jq.
names_issue=$(tehas_names_issue)
# This issue's pull request in each state, its address or nothing, and 1 when
# a pull request of the bot on the branch names another issue and none names
# this one: that one is said, not taken.
mapfile -d '' -t got < <(jq -j --arg n "$issue" "$one$names_issue"'
  def url_in($st): [.[] | select(type == "object" and ((.state // "") | ascii_upcase) == $st) | select(mine)
    | .url | strings | select(test("^https://[A-Za-z0-9./_-]+\\z"))] | first // "";
  (url_in("OPEN") | v), (url_in("MERGED") | v), (url_in("CLOSED") | v),
  ([.[] | select(type == "object") | mine] | if any(not) and (any | not) then 1 else 0 end | v)' <<<"$prs" 2>/dev/null)
pr=${got[0]-} merged_pr=${got[1]-} closed_pr=${got[2]-} foreign=""
[ "${got[3]-}" != 1 ] ||
  foreign=" The branch \`${branch//[!A-Za-z0-9_.\/-]/}\` has a pull request of the bot that does not name this issue, so it is not taken as this cycle's."
# Said in the comment when the label stayed: the issue then reads as running.
stuck=" The label of this issue could not be changed. The next poll of the dispatcher hands it to a human."

summary="status \`$status\`, last step \`$step\`, $mins min"
# A reader of the comment is told where the step came from: only the log of a
# runner that wrote no result can name the step it died in, and a step read
# from a log is the runner's own word, not the verdict of its result.
[ "$step_from" = log ] && [ "$step" != - ] &&
  summary="status \`$status\`, last step \`$step\` read from its run log, $mins min"
[ "$step_from" = stalled ] && [ "$step" != - ] &&
  summary="status \`$status\`, last step \`$step\` tehas work saw in flight, $mins min"
[ "$step_from" = timed ] && [ "$step" != - ] &&
  summary="status \`$status\`, last step \`$step\` tehas work saw in flight, $mins min"
[ "$step_from" = nostart ] && [ "$step" != - ] &&
  summary="status \`$status\`, no step started, the container of the cycle ended before the driver wrote an event, $mins min"
# Why the cycle stopped, for a comment: its cap and, for an intent park, the
# reason. A cap is a word of the runner's published vocabulary, or one of
# tehas_cycle_own_cap, the caps tehas sets for a stop at its own bound.
why=""
if tehas_cycle_own_cap "${cap:-}"; then
  why=" tehas stopped the cycle: the cap \`$cap\`."
else
  [ -z "$cap" ] || why=" pi-rukas stopped on the cap \`$cap\`${park_reason:+ (\`$park_reason\`)}."
fi
[ "$cap" != deadline ] || why="$why It reached its deadline before the time limit and started no new step."
# The failure behind the cap, where the result gives one. Of kind cap nothing
# failed: the cap's own rule stopped the cycle, and the cap says that.
if [ -z "$own" ] && [ "$failure_from" = result ] && [ -n "$failure_kind" ] && [ "$failure_kind" != cap ] && [ "$provider" != 1 ]; then
  why="$why It failed: kind \`$failure_kind\`${failure_role:+, role \`$failure_role\`}${failure_cause:+, cause \`$failure_cause\`}${reason:+: $reason}."
fi
marker=""
[ -z "$handoff" ] || marker="<!-- pi-rukas:handoff issue=$issue cap=${cap:-none} -->"
# Why the dispatcher stopped this cycle, if it did.
done_why=""
IFS= LC_ALL=C read -r -n 16 done_why 2>/dev/null <"$cycles/$issue.done"
case "$done_why" in closed | merged | pr-closed) ;; quiet) [ -n "$park_reason" ] || park_reason=quiet; why="$why The dispatcher stopped it: nothing was heard from it for longer than its quiet bound." ;; *) done_why="" ;; esac
rm -f "$cycles/$issue.done"
# The approval this start used, read once for the report: the pull request a
# refresh marked. A start by hand used none. An approval that cannot be read
# leaves it unknown: the mark of a refresh is then the dispatcher's to clear.
# A refresh is on record at the time the poll decided it: the person's approval
# it rests on is the one named for it, as approval-view names it.
refreshed="" base_at="" base_by=""
if tehas_store approval-current "$issue"; then
  read -r refreshed base_at base_by < <(jq -r --argjson a "$attempt" '.rests_on as $r
    | .current | select(. != null and .used_by == $a)
    | [(if .how == "refresh" then (.refresh_pr // .input.refresh_of.pr) else null end), $r.at, $r.by]
    | map(. // "-" | tostring) | join(" ")' <<<"$tehas_store_out" 2>/dev/null)
else
  log "WARN: the approval of #$issue could not be read for its report ($tehas_store_word)"
fi
lean_note=${cycles:?}/${issue:?}.lean-refused
if [ "$rc" = 78 ] && [ -f "$lean_note" ] && [ ! -L "$lean_note" ]; then rc_start=4; else rc_start=$rc; fi
[[ $refreshed =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,9}$ ]] || refreshed=""
[[ $base_at =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,10}$ ]] || base_at=""
# park <text>: hand the issue to a human with the retry question.
# park_why is provider-outage only for the park of a provider outage; any
# other park is on record by its cap, else its status.
park_why=""
# parked_auto: what an outage park says it does on its own, once it is on record.
parked_auto=""
parked_now=false
never_ran=0
# A runner that wrote no result, whose branch GitHub says carries the bot's
# open pull request: commit-pr got that far and the review after it did not.
# Said with the pull request rather than as a cycle that left nothing. The one
# decision is made here, where the log was read and its words are still in
# scope: `log_words` is 1 only for a log the helper opened and read, and
# `log_read` says whether it read the whole of it. A run whose log is bigger
# than the 200 KB tehas reads may have lost its `commit-pr` line at the cut, so
# the branch of the run and the pull request GitHub lists may then name two
# things: such a cycle keeps the park's own wording, which tells a person to
# look for the pull request before they retry. A result that was written, a
# cycle tehas work never watched, and a cycle whose log the helper could not
# open are each reported the way they were before. GitHub said the pull request
# is here; the log only named the branch to ask about, so no line of a log
# reports one GitHub does not list.
died_with_open_pr=0
if [ "$log_words" = 1 ] && [ "$log_read" = whole ] && [ -n "$pr" ] &&
  [ -z "$merged_pr" ] && [ -z "$closed_pr" ]; then
  died_with_open_pr=1
fi
park() {
  parked_now=true
  relabel --remove-label tehas:running --add-label needs-human-attention && stuck=""
  local parked="$1" park_marker question="" on_record=0 why last repeat
  local -a park_args
  # The fields of the history line written below, for a fixer that reads GitHub.
  park_marker=$(tehas_park_marker "$issue" "$step" "$cap" "$park_reason" "$failure_kind" "$failure_cause" "$provider") ||
    park_marker=""
  # The park repeats the issue's previous end when that line is a park of this
  # one's sig and of no provider outage: 1 for the first such repeat, and tehas
  # history counts the run on its own read. Unreadable history says nothing.
  repeat=""
  [ -n "$park_marker" ] && [ "$provider" != 1 ] &&
    repeat=$(tehas_park_repeat "$issue" "$park_marker")
  # provider-outage is tehas's word alone: the store owes a retry for it. A
  # cap or status is the container's, so it never takes that word.
  why=${park_why:-${cap:-${status:-unknown}}}
  [ -n "$park_why" ] || [ "$why" != provider-outage ] || why="cap:provider-outage"
  park_args=("$issue" --attempt "$attempt" --reason "$why")
  [ -z "$step" ] || park_args+=(--step "$step")
  case "${pr:-}" in
    "https://github.com/$repo/pull/"*)
      [[ ! ${pr##*/} =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,9}$ ]] || park_args+=(--pr "${pr##*/}")
      ;;
  esac
  # On record before the issue is told: a question whose park is not on record
  # would offer boxes that start nothing.
  [ -z "$shadow" ] || printf '%s\0' "${park_args[@]}" >"$shadow.park"
  if tehas_store_try 3 park "${park_args[@]}"; then
    on_record=1
  else
    log "WARN: could not record the park of #$issue ($tehas_store_word: $tehas_store_line), so it asks no question"
  fi
  # A develop timeout leaves a note in the body, which the next cycle reads; a
  # second one on the same text offers no retry (#832, #833).
  retry=true
  if [ "$cap" = child-timeout:develop ] && [ "$signalled" = 0 ] && last=$(timeout 30 gh api "repos/$repo/issues/$issue" 2>/dev/null |
    timeout 30 "$TEHAS_HOME/libexec/tehas-cycle" last-attempt "$issue" "$result" "$workdir" 2>/dev/null); then
    [ "$(jq -r .action <<<"$last")" != repeat ] || { retry=false && parked+=" $(jq -r .say <<<"$last")"; }
    [ "$(jq -r .action <<<"$last")" != edit ] || jq '{body}' <<<"$last" | gh_wait=30 gh_call api -X PATCH "repos/$repo/issues/$issue" --input - >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
      log "WARN: could not leave the last attempt's note in the body of #$issue"
  fi
  if [ -n "$parked_auto" ]; then
    if [ "$on_record" = 1 ]; then
      parked+=$parked_auto
    else
      parked+=" Its park could not be recorded, so it waits for a person."
    fi
  fi
  parked+=$stuck
  # The host's line above the boxes, when this park repeats the park before it.
  [ -n "$repeat" ] && parked+=$(q_park_repeat "$repeat" "$step" "$cap")
  # The body q_render posts, in a file of its own that goes when it is posted.
  if [ "$on_record" = 1 ]; then
    [ -e "$TEHAS_HOME/tmp" ] || mkdir -m 700 "$TEHAS_HOME/tmp" 2>/dev/null
    question=$(mktemp "$TEHAS_HOME/tmp/park-question.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null) || question=""
    if [ -n "$question" ] && ! jq -n --arg text "$parked${handoff:+

$handoff}

${usage}" --arg marker "$marker" --arg park "$park_marker" --argjson retry "$retry" '{text: $text, question: null, marker: $marker, park: $park, retry: $retry}' \
      >"$question" 2>/dev/null; then
      rm -f "$question"
      question=""
    fi
  fi
  if [ -n "$question" ] && post_question "$question"; then
    # On record before the label shows it: a tick counts only in the question
    # on record. Without the record a tick starts nothing and says so.
    tehas_store_try 3 question-asked "$issue" --id "$posted" --kind park --body-sha "$posted_sum" ||
      log "WARN: could not record the park question of #$issue, its boxes will not work ($tehas_store_word)"
    # A separate call: a repository without this label must still get the
    # parking label above. The poll only reads questions on issues that
    # carry this label, so without it the boxes do nothing: say so.
    if ! relabel --add-label tehas:question; then
      if ! tehas_store_try 3 question-answered "$issue" --id "$posted" --withdraw && [ "$tehas_store_word" != unknown ]; then
        log "WARN: could not take the park question of #$issue off the record ($tehas_store_word)"
      fi
      comment "The boxes above cannot be used: the label \`tehas:question\` could not be added (run \`tehas labels\` on the host). To retry, apply \`tehas:ready\`."
    fi
  else
    # Without the boxes there is nothing to tick: say the old way.
    comment "${marker:+$marker
}$(printf '%s' "$parked To retry, apply \`tehas:ready\`.${handoff:+

$handoff}

${usage}" | q_clean 60000)${park_marker:+

$park_marker}"
  fi
  [ -z "$question" ] || rm -f "$question"
}
log_where="the run log is \`~/tehas/logs/dispatch-work-$issue.log\` on \`$HOSTNAME\`"
# Said wherever a stopped cycle's workspace stayed on the host, with the path a
# person has to open to read it. Named in ~ form as the manual and the log line
# name it: these comments go to a public issue. A host whose TEHAS_HOME is not
# under HOME gets the path as it is. tehas work can move an earlier attempt
# aside and then fail before it clones, so the only copy left may be the one
# beside the workspace: name it too, or this comment says there is nothing to
# recover when there is. Nothing replaces that copy on its own. A retry that
# clones no workspace leaves it where it is, until a tidy or the poll's sweep.
if [ -d "$workdir" ]; then
  kept_at=$workdir
elif [ -d "$workdir.before-retry" ]; then
  kept_at=$workdir.before-retry
else
  kept_at=""
fi
if [ -n "$HOME" ] && [ "$kept_at" != "${kept_at#"$HOME"/}" ]; then
  kept_at="~${kept_at#"$HOME"}"
fi
if [ -n "$kept_at" ]; then
  kept="The workspace is kept at \`$kept_at\`"
else
  kept="This cycle left no workspace on this host"
fi
if [ -n "$merged_pr" ]; then
  # A merge ends the cycle, whoever made it and whatever the state file says:
  # a person may merge while the cycle still reviews. tehasbot status labels
  # the closed issue as shipped.
  relabel --remove-label tehas:running && stuck=""
  if [ "$rc" = 0 ] && [ "$status" = merged ]; then
    comment "Tehas cycle finished: $summary. The change was merged: $merged_pr$killed$stuck"
    tidy
  else
    # The merge came from outside while the cycle still worked. What it pushed
    # after that reaches nothing, and what it had not pushed is only here.
    comment "Tehas cycle ended: its pull request $merged_pr was merged while the cycle ran (last step \`$step\`, $mins min, runner exit $runner_rc). Nothing more is needed here. $kept.$killed$stuck"
  fi
  status=merged
  pr=$merged_pr
elif [ "$unread" = 1 ]; then
  # Whether the cycle left a pull request is not known. The branch stays on
  # record, with the end, for whoever looks again.
  park "Tehas cycle stopped, and its pull request could not be looked up: GitHub did not list the pull requests of the branch \`$(tr -cd 'A-Za-z0-9_./-' <<<"$branch")\` ($summary, runner exit $runner_rc). Look for one there before you retry. $kept; $log_where.$killed"
elif [ "$rc" = 0 ] && [ "$status" = merged ]; then
  # A claim the container wrote. Without the merge to show for it the
  # workspaces are kept: what they hold may exist nowhere else.
  park "Tehas cycle stopped: its state says \`merged\`, but the bot has no merged pull request for this issue on \`$(tr -cd 'A-Za-z0-9_./-' <<<"${branch:-none}")\` ($summary). Nothing was removed; $log_where.$killed${pr:+ Its branch has an open pull request: $pr}$foreign"
elif { [ "$rc" = 10 ] || [ "$rc" = 11 ]; } && [ "$status" = handoff ] && [ -n "$pr" ] &&
  [ "$cap" = awaiting-human-merge ] && [ "$review_held" = false ] && attention_off; then
  relabel --remove-label tehas:running --add-label tehas:awaiting-merge && stuck=""
  comment "Tehas cycle finished: $summary. Pull request waiting for a human: $pr$killed$stuck"
  gh_call pr comment "$pr" --body "${marker:+$marker
}$(printf '%s' "Tehas cycle for #$issue: $summary.${handoff:+

$handoff}

${usage:-No token usage was recorded for this cycle.}" | q_clean 60000)" >/dev/null 2>&1 || log "WARN: comment on $pr failed"
  tidy
elif { [ "$rc" = 10 ] || [ "$rc" = 11 ]; } && [ "$status" = handoff ] && [ -n "$pr" ]; then
  # A handoff with its pull request open for any other reason is not waiting
  # only for a merge: an incomplete review, a cap, a reason that cannot be
  # read, a review whose round cap left findings open, or a label a person or
  # pi-rukas put on the issue. It is parked, and the pull request is named.
  held=""
  [ "$review_held" = false ] || [ "$cap" != awaiting-human-merge ] ||
    held=" Its review ran out of rounds with findings still open, so it is not waiting only for a merge."
  [ -n "$cap" ] || why=" The result names no cap, so it is not taken for a pull request that waits only for a merge."
  park "Tehas cycle handed off with its pull request open: $summary (runner exit $runner_rc).$why$held Read the pull request before you retry: $pr. $kept; $log_where.$killed$foreign"
elif [ "$done_why" = closed ]; then
  # Closed while the cycle ran: nobody is waiting on it, so nothing is asked.
  # The workspace stays for the sweep.
  relabel --remove-label tehas:running && stuck=""
  comment "Tehas stopped the cycle: this issue was closed while it ran ($summary). Nothing more is needed here. $kept; $log_where.$killed$stuck"
  status=closed
elif [ -z "$pr" ] && [ -n "$closed_pr" ] && [ "$rc_start" != 4 ]; then
  # A person closed the pull request without merging it: whether to try again
  # is theirs to say, with the label. No retry box.
  relabel --remove-label tehas:running --add-label needs-human-attention && stuck=""
  comment "${marker:+$marker
}$(printf '%s' "Tehas cycle stopped: its pull request $closed_pr was closed without being merged ($summary, runner exit $runner_rc).$why $kept; $log_where.$killed$stuck To retry, apply \`tehas:ready\`.${handoff:+

$handoff}" | q_clean 60000)"
elif [ "$died_with_open_pr" = 1 ]; then
  # A handoff to a person, not a cycle that left nothing: the change is up for
  # review and a clean retry would redo it, so it is parked with no retry box.
  relabel --remove-label tehas:running --add-label needs-human-attention && stuck=""
  comment "${marker:+$marker
}$(printf '%s' "Tehas cycle handed off with its pull request open and its review unfinished: $summary (runner exit $runner_rc). Its runner wrote no result, so tehas read its run log for the step it was in and GitHub for the bot's pull request on its branch: $pr. Read the pull request before you retry: a clean start redoes what it already holds. $kept; $log_where.$killed$stuck" | q_clean 60000)"
  # On record as the handoff it was reported as, with the pull request found.
  status=handoff
else
  parked="Tehas cycle stopped without a pull request: $summary (runner exit $runner_rc).$why${handoff:+ The handoff of pi-rukas follows.} $kept; $log_where.$killed${pr:+ Its branch has an open pull request: $pr}$foreign"
  if [ "$rc_start" = 4 ]; then
    # What is known of a cycle that never ran is the dispatcher's record of the
    # start, and the log tehas work wrote its reason into.
    never_ran=1
    # The time a person approved: a refresh names the approval it rests on,
    # and the retry after an outage is the poll's own, so it names no time.
    approved_by=$start_by approved_epoch=$approval_at
    case $start_how in
      refresh) approved_by=$base_by approved_epoch=$base_at ;;
      outage-retry) approved_epoch="" ;;
    esac
    [[ $approved_by =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,39}$ ]] || approved_by=""
    approved_at=""
    [ -z "$approved_epoch" ] || approved_at=$(date -u -d "@$approved_epoch" +%FT%TZ 2>/dev/null) || approved_at=""
    never_ran_said=""
    if [ -n "$approved_by" ] && [ -n "$approved_at" ]; then
      never_ran_said=" @${approved_by} approved this start at ${approved_at}; tehas keeps no record of an approval that started nothing, so a fresh \`tehas:ready\` asks for another start."
    elif [ -n "$approved_by" ]; then
      never_ran_said=" @${approved_by} approved this start; tehas keeps no record of an approval that started nothing, so a fresh \`tehas:ready\` asks for another start."
    else
      never_ran_said=" No approval of this start is recorded on this host: a fresh \`tehas:ready\` is needed."
    fi
    parked="Tehas started no cycle: an image or file the issue links could not be given to it, or the issue could not be read for them (runner exit $runner_rc). Nothing of it ran: no workspace, no container, no step, no usage.$never_ran_said The comment before this one names it; $log_where."
    [ "$rc" = 4 ] || parked="Tehas started no cycle: this start's approval says driver \`lean\`, and this tehas cannot run the lean driver yet (runner exit $runner_rc). It does not run classic in its place, and nothing of it ran. To run it on classic, take \`tehas:lean\` off, then retry; $log_where."
  elif [ "$provider" = 1 ]; then
    park_why="provider-outage"
    parked="Tehas cycle parked by a provider outage: $summary (runner exit $runner_rc), cap \`$cap\`${reason:+: $reason}; $log_where.$killed${pr:+ Its branch has an open pull request: $pr}$foreign"
    # What the store derives for the park: one start on the dispatcher's own,
    # resting on the account that started this cycle, which a run by hand has
    # not got.
    if [ "$requeued" = 1 ]; then
      parked="$parked It was the retry tehas made on its own after an outage, so it waits for a person."
    elif [ "$start_how" = hand ] || [ -z "$start_by" ]; then
      parked="$parked It was started by hand, so it waits for a person."
    else
      # Said only of a park the store took: none on record owes a retry.
      parked_auto=" Tehas starts it again once on its own, when the model endpoint answers."
    fi
  fi
  park "$parked"
fi
# A refresh marked its pull request tehas:refreshing as it started. The mark
# goes whatever the outcome; one that stays is the dispatcher's to clear.
if [ -n "$refreshed" ]; then
  for ((try = 1; try <= tries; try++)); do
    # The REST issues endpoint: `gh pr edit` asks GitHub a GraphQL question
    # the bot's token may not answer (issue #120). A 404 says the label is not
    # there, which is what this wants, so it counts as done.
    if gh_label_off "repos/$repo/issues/$refreshed/labels/tehas:refreshing"; then
      break
    fi
    if [ "$try" = "$tries" ]; then
      log "WARN: label change on pull request #$refreshed failed: --remove-label tehas:refreshing"
    else
      sleep "$retry_wait"
    fi
  done
fi
log "done #$issue rc=$rc status=$status step=$step minutes=$mins pr=${pr:-none} oom_kills=$kills forks_refused=$forks"
# A person closing the pull request is a park too: it needs a person.
[ -z "$closed_pr" ] || [ -n "$pr" ] || [ -n "$merged_pr" ] || [ "$status" = closed ] || parked_now=true
# The end on the cycle's record, with its one row of history, once the issue
# has been told: what it says is what the comments said. The result and the
# progress go as tehas work left them; the store keeps each only when it can
# read it, and refuses a link. The pull request is kept by its number, and
# only one of this repository's.
end_args=("$issue" --attempt "$attempt" --status "$status" --step "$step" --rc "$rc" --oom "$kills" --forks "$forks")
[ "$watched" != 1 ] || end_args+=(--runner-exit "$runner_rc")
[ "$work_status" != killed ] || end_args+=(--host-saw killed)
[ "$stalled" = 0 ] || end_args+=(--stalled "$stalled")
[ "$timed" = 0 ] || end_args+=(--timed)
[ -z "$branch" ] || end_args+=(--branch "$branch")
case "${pr:-}" in
  "https://github.com/$repo/pull/"*)
    pr_number=${pr##*/}
    [[ ! $pr_number =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,9}$ ]] || end_args+=(--pr "$pr_number")
    ;;
esac
[ -z "${pr_number:-}" ] || title_why=$(timeout -k 5 "$((signalled ? stop_gh_wait : 120))" "$console" pr-title sync "$pr_number" 2>&1) || log "WARN: the title of pull request #$pr_number was not kept: ${title_why:-it took too long}"
for f in result progress; do
  [ ! -e "$cycles/$issue.$f.json" ] && [ ! -L "$cycles/$issue.$f.json" ] ||
    end_args+=("--$f" "$cycles/$issue.$f.json")
done
[ -z "$cap" ] || end_args+=(--cap "$cap")
[ -z "$park_reason" ] || end_args+=(--park-reason "$park_reason")
[ -z "$failure_kind" ] || end_args+=(--failure-kind "$failure_kind")
[ -z "$failure_cause" ] || end_args+=(--failure-cause "$failure_cause")
[ "$provider" != 1 ] || end_args+=(--provider)
[ "$parked_now" != true ] || end_args+=(--parked)
# A start that never ran takes its approval with it, once analytics has read it:
# the fixer, a refresh and a later start read an approval as one whose cycle happened.
approved=$("$tc" approved "$issue" "$attempt") || approved=null
[ "$never_ran" != 1 ] || end_args+=(--never-ran)
[ -z "$shadow" ] || printf '%s\0' "${end_args[@]}" >"$shadow.end"
if tehas_store_try 5 cycle-end "${end_args[@]}"; then
  # What the store would not keep of what tehas work left: a link, a file over
  # its bound or not JSON, and progress older than this start.
  for f in result progress; do
    kept=$(tehas_store_get ".$f | strings")
    case "$kept" in refused | too-large | unreadable | stale) log "WARN: the $f of #$issue is not on record: $kept" ;; esac
  done
else end_rc=70
  log "ERROR: the end of #$issue is not on record ($tehas_store_word: $tehas_store_line): cycle-end ${end_args[*]}"
fi
[ -z "$shadow" ] || timeout 60 "$tc" compare "$issue" >/dev/null 2>&1 || log "WARN: the report's shadow of #$issue could not be compared"
# What follows the end, each step's failure a WARN line (tehas-cycle after).
"$tc" after "$issue" < <(printf '%s\0' --attempt "$attempt" --started "$started" --rc "$rc" --status "$status" --step "$step" --cap "$cap" --park-reason "$park_reason" --failure-kind "$failure_kind" --failure-cause "$failure_cause" --provider "$provider" --parked "$parked_now" --pr "${pr:-}" --by "$start_by" --how "$start_how" --approved "$approved" --image "$image_id" --image-built "$image_built" --rukas-image "$image_rukas" --oom "$kills" --forks "$forks" --reason "$reason" --build "$build" --signalled "$signalled" --workdir "$workdir" --console "$console" --status-cmd "$status_cmd") || log "WARN: what follows the end of #$issue did not run"
exit "${end_rc:-0}"
